{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-115931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/4f9f16654bc92f01c9cda8308ab4679695ed7d5055a12f6963303d4071c95bea.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-115931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/0234179c0a2dfb514a921f19e6fdc7bcad022aa0368d122a7d2ecc0e5d27df73.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}(2-y) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(2-y) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-115931" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1d87e06d5f6add18bcbe2dfa468a4b25c5109a0698e09ecaf60abe4d55a18c19"} {"question" "q-115931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-115931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=-y(y+2)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=y(2-y)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(y+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-115931" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-116024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/660feebe44e179b53be85d23c497e58de4774258b588a6f8ac785a0fd9c8fc08.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2y-{y}^{2}-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{y-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-116024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/a76856ea0a6b50b821ba6c39e6a87a5acf5aabd704454b4875540b3970f3d249.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are negative everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2y-{y}^{2}-1}=-\\frac{1}{(y-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is negative for all $y\\ne 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}=-\\frac{y+1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to ∓\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for $y>1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-116024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1d87e06d5f6add18bcbe2dfa468a4b25c5109a0698e09ecaf60abe4d55a18c19"} {"question" "q-116024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-116024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-116024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/8d2588ba2b8987841a34e981aa6b7d48cdc41bebfca6c0517cc9cf67d788f591.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/1b5303303f0aea22149b3d418e08724cfea9cf64943eab978fe38c0d025d73bf.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-1)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-1) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)^{2}(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1d87e06d5f6add18bcbe2dfa468a4b25c5109a0698e09ecaf60abe4d55a18c19"} {"question" "q-120150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(y-1)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=y-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/e0da4ce40e23a44ddfd946b9a9bf00882ca0a83db8379c6492b58355772c6006.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/ddac2bab6fb72979e1142179dfb11792cc50044de545131139edfdeff9215e3c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=\\pm 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}=(2+y)(2-y)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4=(y+2)(y-2).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}=(2+y)(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1d87e06d5f6add18bcbe2dfa468a4b25c5109a0698e09ecaf60abe4d55a18c19"} {"question" "q-120174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=4y-{y}^{3}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-4y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/1a3ba94aa37bd8b8494794a7013956b100f418c9e0aa7efecab5ba6fc6476ff6.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/assets/1a3ba94aa37bd8b8494794a7013956b100f418c9e0aa7efecab5ba6fc6476ff6.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(y+1)(y-1)} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{(1+y)(1-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}$ do *not* become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1d87e06d5f6add18bcbe2dfa468a4b25c5109a0698e09ecaf60abe4d55a18c19"} {"question" "q-120196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{y-1}{{y}^{2}+1}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=y({y}^{2}-1)"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"} {"question" "q-120196" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064933/state.json" "file_sha256" "a9a247c3ce081e74038fd138fba51884d73cf37b75fa9c78ededafc2639bd401"}] "usage" {"q-116024" []} "basis" 2126 "retractions" [[17592186167020 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/q-116024.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{2y - y^{2} - 1}$\n2. $y^{′} =-\\frac{y^{2} + y}{y - 1}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{3}{y - 1}$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163539 17592186163540 17592186163541 17592186163542 17592186163543] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163538 :answer-field/correct] [17592186163538 :answer-field/id] [17592186163538 :answer-field/key] [17592186163538 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
